  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 254 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: After a stretch of dry and seasonable weather the last \nfew days, shower chances will return later tonight through the \nweekend. The main feature driving precipitation chances is a large \nupper level trough approaching from the Great Lakes. A frontal \nboundary associated with the parent low will move through tomorrow \nafternoon, driving the chances for scattered showers and \nthunderstorms through the afternoon. Model guidance shows modest \ninstability tomorrow afternoon, with CAPE values of 500 J/kg or so \ndepending on the location, although there is some uncertainty as to \nhow much cloud cover lingers through the morning and inhibits \nafternoon heating. While it's not the most impressive environment, \nthere should be enough instability, shear, and forcing to drive \nthunderstorm development tomorrow afternoon. SPC currently shows \nnearly all of the forecast area in a Marginal Risk for severe \nweather tomorrow, meaning that a few isolated strong to severe \nthunderstorms are possible, with possible hail and strong winds as \nthe primary threats. The most likely time frame for thunderstorms \nwill be from noon through about 7PM, so be sure to monitor the \nforecast if you have any outdoor plans. Storms looks to be \nprogressive, limiting any hydro concerns at this time, especially \ngiven the dry weather the last few days. Current rainfall forecasts \ngenerally show about 0.25 to 0.50 inches of rain across the area, \nwith lower amounts near the International Border and higher amounts \npossible within any thunderstorms. \n\nFor the start of the week, the region will remain in broad cyclonic \nflow, will will allow for linger showers on Monday and Tuesday, \nespecially across the higher terrain. This cyclonic flow will also \nlead to some lake effect showers off Lake Ontario, with increased \nshower chances expected across northern New York during this time \nperiod. Temperatures will be on the cool side, with highs only \nclimbing into the 70s, with some locations in the Adirondacks \nstruggling to climb out of the upper 60s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Long wave troughing appears to remain over the eastern \nUS later next week, with the base of the trough to our west. As \nsuch, the pattern will be somewhat unsettled with chances for \nshowers returning late in the week. The low pressure track is \nstrongly favored to be well to our north into central or northern \nQuebec, so widespread heavy rain or strong wind is not on the table, \nand deep layer shear appears limited for severe storms at this time. \nOf the latest ensemble scenarios, ones with a bit more amplified \ntrough than the mean upper level heights show 0-500 millibar shear \nprobabilities exceeding 30 knots at a greater than 30% chance \n(rather than close to 0%). \n\nProbable hazards for this system would be fairly widespread, general \nthunderstorm potential associated with the system's cold front, \nwhich may be thermodynamically strong. Machine learning and other \nensemble guidance does suggest seasonably high instability for late \nAugust could be present, with a slight preference for stronger \nvariety thunderstorms as you go southward (roughly 5% chance of \nsevere weather per GEFS). With a faster timing of an incoming cold \nfront, this threat may be limited to Thursday, and a slower timing \nwould suggest Friday would be a day to watch for thunderstorms, \nespecially in Vermont. The latest guidance has trended perhaps a \nlittle more towards the later timing, consistent with an occluding \nlow pressure system and associated slowing eastward progress of a \ncold front. For now, a slight chance of thunderstorms Thursday \nevening, and slight chance to chance Friday afternoon in most of the \nregion, seems reasonable at this point. Given the amount of outdoor \nactivities anticipated on Friday and large scale setup for \nthunderstorm potential, this will be a period to keep a close eye.",
